Yes. Looking at your code, I would make a few changes before starting the fine-tuning run. Your vLLM inference issue is separate; this training script has a couple of things that are more important to fix.

1. Most important: don’t train on the entire conversation

You currently have:

tokens["labels"] = tokens["input_ids"].copy()

This means the loss is calculated on every token, including the user/instruction part.

For a chat/safety dataset, you generally want:

Input: conversation/question
Target: assistant’s safety response

rather than teaching the model to reproduce the entire conversation.

If your JSON looks like:

{
  "messages": [
    {"role": "user", "content": "..."},
    {"role": "assistant", "content": "..."}
  ]
}

I recommend masking the user/system tokens with -100 and calculating loss only on assistant tokens.

This is more important than the CUDA issue you just solved.

⸻

2. Your current training is full fine-tuning

You have:

model = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(
    MODEL_NAME,
    torch_dtype="auto",
    device_map="auto",
    trust_remote_code=True
)

There is no LoRA/PEFT here.

So you’re doing full fine-tuning of Qwen3-8B.

If you’re on an A100 80GB, this may work, but it is considerably more memory-hungry than LoRA.

Your current:

per_device_train_batch_size=4
gradient_accumulation_steps=8

gives an effective batch size of:

4 × 8 = 32

That’s reasonable, but batch size 4 at sequence length 1024 during full 8B training can still be tight depending on your exact GPU and Transformers/PyTorch configuration.

I would add:

gradient_checkpointing=True,
bf16=True,

and preferably:

gradient_checkpointing_kwargs={"use_reentrant": False},

This substantially reduces activation memory.

⸻

3. Don’t use device_map="auto" for training

For training, I’d change:

device_map="auto"

to simply:

device_map=None

or omit it.

device_map="auto" is primarily useful for inference/model placement and can cause undesirable behavior with training/Trainer.

For a single A100, let Trainer/PyTorch place the model on the GPU.

So:

model = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(
    MODEL_NAME,
    torch_dtype=torch.bfloat16,
    trust_remote_code=True
)

⸻

4. Use BF16 explicitly

Instead of:

torch_dtype="auto"

I’d use:

torch_dtype=torch.bfloat16

and:

bf16=True,
fp16=False,

assuming your A100 supports BF16 — it does.

This gives you a more predictable training configuration.

⸻

5. Your learning rate is reasonable

You currently have:

learning_rate=2e-4

For full fine-tuning of an 8B model, however, I would be more conservative.

I’d start with:

learning_rate=1e-5

or:

learning_rate=2e-5

2e-4 is much more aggressive for full-model fine-tuning.

If this were LoRA, 2e-4 would be much more normal.

For your current full fine-tuning:

I’d use 2e-5.

⸻

6. Add warmup

I don’t see a warmup setting in your screenshot.

Add:

warmup_ratio=0.05,

This means the learning rate gradually ramps up during the first 5% of training.

For safety-oriented fine-tuning, this is a good precaution against large early updates.

⸻

7. Your 3 epochs are okay

You have:

num_train_epochs=3

That’s reasonable.

But because you’re fine-tuning an 8B model for a safety dataset, I would watch validation loss carefully.

You already have:

eval_dataset=dataset["validation"]

which is good.

I’d also enable:

load_best_model_at_end=True,
metric_for_best_model="eval_loss",
greater_is_better=False,

Then the final model will be the checkpoint with the lowest validation loss, rather than simply the model from the end of epoch 3.

⸻

8. Change your saving/evaluation strategy slightly

You currently have:

evaluation_strategy="steps",
eval_steps=100,
save_steps=100,
save_total_limit=2,

That’s fine.

I’d add:

load_best_model_at_end=True,
metric_for_best_model="eval_loss",
greater_is_better=False,

And make sure save_steps and eval_steps are the same — yours already are.

